          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Laurent series
            generalize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            by allowing both positive and negative powers. In particular,
            %em Laurent series
            with complex coefficients generalize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
            of analytic functions to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meromorphic+functions"} meromorphic functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z)
            at finite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\in\mathbb{C}
            has the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                    %mn ∞
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z) = \sum_{n=k}^{\infty}f_n z^n,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is merely constrained to be finite and is often negative.
          %p
            Or, in some contexts one wants to take
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = -\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} R \cdot z^n
            of a ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} R \cdot z^n
            (see Laurent polynomials below). In such contexts, Laurent series can be likened to distributions, i.e., functionals on the algebra of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \to R
            with compact support.
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em Laurent series
              in one variable
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
              over a commutative unital ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubly+infinite+series"} doubly infinite series
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn ∞
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z) = \sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty} f_n z^n ,
            %p
              where
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n\in k
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo →
                      %mi k
                      %mo :
                      %mi n
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \to k: n \mapsto f_n
              = succeed "-module" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi z
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [z, z^{-1}] ]
          %p
            A
            %em Laurent polynomial
            is a Laurent series for which all but finitely many
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n
            are zero. Laurent polynomials form a ring which may be described as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[z, z^{-1}]
            or abstractly as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[x, y]/(x y - 1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(z) = f(z)g(z)
            of two Laurent series
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z), g(z)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mi k
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_n = \sum_{k \in \mathbb{Z}} f_k g_{n-k},
          %p
            doesn’t make sense in general (although it could sometimes make sense in topological contexts where some such infinite sums can converge, as in the case
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = \mathbb{C}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The
              = succeed "-vector" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi z
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z) \in k[z, z^{-1}]
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(z)
              is a Laurent series, then the product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(z) = f(z)g(z)
              as defined above always makes sense.
          %p
            In general, questions of convergence are treated as separate issues. In complex analysis, the Laurent series
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} a_n z^n
            describes a meromorphic function in a neighborhood around the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = 0
            (possibly with a pole there) if all but finitely many negatively indexed terms are zero. Similarly, series of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi N
                    %mn ∞
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo −
                  %mi a
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{n = -N}^{\infty} a_n (z-a)^n
            describe meromorphic functions in a neighborhood of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z=a
            with poles of order at most
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %p
            On the other hand, in algebra one often hears of the ring of
            = succeed "." do
              %em formal Laurent series
          #ring.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em ring of formal Laurent series
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in an indeterminate
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              consists of Laurent series
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℤ
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} f_n z^n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n \in A
              but where all but finitely many
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \lt 0
              vanish.
          %p
            Multiplication defined as above clearly makes sense. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k((x))
            and is in fact a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+of+fractions"} field of fractions
            of the ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [x] ]
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[ [x] ]
            is often viewed as a discrete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valuation+ring"} valuation ring
          #restrict.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              It would perhaps be clearer if we used the term “restricted Laurent series” to cover the Laurent series considered in Definition
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ring"}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#laurent_series_as_distributions Laurent series as distributions
          %p Another point of view on Laurent series is given in the following alternative definition.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A Laurent series over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              is a
              = succeed "-linear" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi z
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: k[z, z^{-1}] \to k
            %p on the algebra of Laurent polynomials.
          %p
            The associated series is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℤ
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} \phi(z^n)z^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo →
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \to k
            with compact (finite) support, via the evident inclusion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo ↪
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi k
                    %mi ℤ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[z, z^{-1}] \cong \oplus_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} k \cdot z^n \hookrightarrow \prod_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} k \cdot z^n = k^{\mathbb{Z}}.
          %p Of particular interest as a Laurent series is the formal Dirac distribution,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(z) = \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} z^n,
          %p
            which intuitively is like the Fourier transform
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn ∞
                    %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi n
                      %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{n = -\infty}^\infty e^{i n x}
            of the pointwise multiplicative identity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}(n) = 1
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z)
              a Laurent polynomial,
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z)\delta(z) = f(1)\delta(z)
          %p
            Indeed, notice that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(z)
            is the distribution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \mapsto p(1)
            which evaluates a Laurent polynomial at the multiplicative identity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z=1
          %p
            More generally, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi k
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in k^\ast
            an invertible unit, the series
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(a z) = \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} a^n z^z
            satisfies
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z)\delta(a z) = f(a^{-1})\delta(a z)
              \.
          %p
            Just as it doesn’t make sense in general to multiply distributions (at least not without heavy qualifications, as in the theory of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Col"} Colombeau
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(z)^2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta'(z) = \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} n z^n
          %p which has the property
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi δ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi δ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi f
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z)\delta'(z) = f(1)\delta'(z) - f'(1)\delta(z)
              \.
          %p
            Indeed, there is a formal calculus of the Dirac distribution that plays an important role in the theory of vertex operator algebras. See for example the treatment in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FLM"} Frenkel, Lepowsky, Meurman
          %p
            One can also work with
            %em convolution products
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(z) = (f \ast g)(z)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(z) = \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} f_n g_n z^n.
          %h3#algebraic_closure Algebraic closure
          #Puiseux.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed
              and has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
              0, then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+closure"} algebraic closure
              of the field of (restricted) Laurent series
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K((x))
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              is the field of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Puiseux+series"} Puiseux series
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            Here “restricted” refers to Remark
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#restrict"}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Puiseux+series"} Puiseux series
            for more details on this result.
